            in the study of continuity.
          %p
            For the general theory of measure spaces, we first need a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+space"} measurable space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \Sigma)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            equipped with a collection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            of
            %strong measurable sets
            complete under certain operations. Then this becomes a measure space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \Sigma, \mu)
            by throwing in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            to a space of values (such as the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            has. If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            is a measurable set, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(E)
            is called the
            %strong measure
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            with respect to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
          %h2#notation Notation
          %p
            The original notation for an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral"} integral
            (going back to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gottfried+Leibniz"} Gottfried Leibniz
          #eq:Leibniz.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_a^b f(x) \,\mathrm{d}x
          %p
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)
            would be replaced by some formula in the variable
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+function"} measurable function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [a,b]
            relative to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+measure"} Lebesgue measure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            and generalise from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [a,b]
            to an arbitrary measurable set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          #eq:full.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi S
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_{x\in{S}} f(x) \,\mu(\mathrm{d}x)
          %p
            instead. Now, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is not given by a formula but rather explicitly named, then there is no need for the dummy variable
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          #eq:simple.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (3)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi S
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi μ
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_S f \,\mu .
          %p
            However, it has been more common to keep the symbol ‘
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d}
            ’ and write
          #eq:excessive.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (4)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi S
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi μ
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_S f \,\mathrm{d}\mu .
          %p
            (Note that ‘
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d}
            ’ can be read as ‘with respect to’ in both
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:Leibniz"} (1)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:excessive"} (4)
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:full"} (2)
            with
          #eq:switched.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (5)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi S
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_{x\in{S}} f(x) \,\mathrm{d}\mu(x) .
          %p
            This last notation, however, hides the fact that integrating a function with respect to a measure is a way of multiplying a function by a measure to get a new measure; the integral of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            with respect to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            is simply the measure of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            with respect to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \mu
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:simple"} (3)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Radon-Nikodym+derivatives"} Radon-Nikodym derivatives
          %p
            It is also possible to take the entire expression ‘
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d}\mu
            ’ as the name of the measure, writing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d}\mu(A)
            even where the common notation is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(A)
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:excessive"} (4)
            is literally the same as (what would otherwise be)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:simple"} (3)
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:switched"} (5)
            is not quite the same as (what would otherwise be)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:full"} (2)
          %p
            We will use
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:simple"} (3)
            below (although other forms may well be found on other pages).
          %p
            See
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://groups.google.com/group/sci.math.research/browse_thread/thread/e28593bfd6b83aac/67a61d19e8f4d57f"} Usenet discussion
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:switched"} (5)
            with the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stieltjes_integral"} Stieltjes integral
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d}x
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:Leibniz"} (1)
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:simple"} (3)
            has also been used in an introductory graduate-level course by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"} John Baez
          %p
            There is also some variation in notation as to whether to use a roman ‘
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d}
            ’ or an italic ‘
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "italic"}
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathit{d}
            ’; roman is more common in England and italic in America. But of course, that variation should not cause any difficulties!
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            A
            %strong measure space
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+space"} measurable space
            equipped with a measure. There are many different kinds of measures; we start with the most specific and then consider generalisations. The motivating example is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+measure"} Lebesgue measure
            on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+interval"} unit interval
          %h3#probability_measures Probability measures
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \Sigma)
            be a measurable space. A
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+measure"} probability measure
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (due to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrey+Kolmogorov"} Andrey Kolmogorov
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            from the collection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            of measurable sets to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+interval"} unit interval
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]
            such that:
          %ol
            %li
              The measure of the entire space is
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/one"} one
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(X) = 1
            %li
              Countable additivity:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mn ∞
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mn ∞
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(\bigcup_{i = 1}^{\infty} S_i) = \sum_{i=1}^{\infty} \mu(S_i)
              whenever the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_i
              are mutually
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+sets"} disjoint
          %p (Part of the latter condition is the requirement that the sum on the right-hand side must converge.)
          %p It is sometimes stated (but in fact follows from the above) that:
          %ul
            %li
              Finitary additivity:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ∪
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(S \cup T) = \mu(S) + \mu(T)
              whenever
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              are disjoint.
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              is increasing:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(A) \leq \mu(B)
              if
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq B
            %li
              The measure of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty set
              is
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero"} zero
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ∅
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(\emptyset) = 0
          %p
            The first of these conditions will follow for all of the generalised notions of measure below, but the second usually will not. Related query discussion is archived
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.ntnu.no/~stacey/Mathforge/nForum/comments.php?DiscussionID=2829&Focus=28141#Comment_28141"} here
          %h3#generalizations Generalizations
          %p
            From now on, we drop the condition
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(X)=1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target"} target
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
          %ul
            %li
              Use
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,\infty)
              (instead of
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]
              = succeed "." do
                %strong positive measure
            %li
              Use
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi R
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{R} = {]{-\infty,\infty}[}
              for a (finite)
              %strong signed measure
              (alias
              = succeed ")." do
                %strong charge
            %li
              Use
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}
              for a (finite)
              = succeed "." do
                %strong complex-valued measure
            %li
              Use an arbitrary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+space"} topological vector space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              for a
              = succeed "." do
                %strong vector-valued measure
            %li
              In principle, one could go further yet;
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              just needs an analogue of addition with a notion of infinitary sum (such as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+abelian+group"} topological abelian group
              has). But until someone suggests a useful example, we will leave this to the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/centipede+mathematics"} centipedes
          %p
            We define an
            %strong infinite measure
            by replacing the domain of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            by an ideal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma'
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            such that the following saturation condition is satisfied: if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{S_i\}_{i\in I}
            is a disjoint family of elements of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma'
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{i\in I}|\mu|(S_i)
            exists (and is finite), then
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcup_{i\in I}S_i\in\Sigma'
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcup_{i\in I}S_i\in\Sigma'
          %p
            An infinite measure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            is
            %strong semifinite
            if for any
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ∖
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S\in\Sigma\setminus\Sigma'
            there is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\in\Sigma'
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\subset S
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(T)\gt0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Radon-Nikodym+theorem"} Radon-Nikodym theorem
            shows that semifinite complex-valued measures that are absolutely continuous with respect to some fixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localizable+measure"} localizable measure
            form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+module"} free module
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            of complex-valued measurable functions (not necessarily bounded).
          %p Some further terms:
          %ul
            %li
              We can combine conditions; for example a
              %strong finite positive measure
              takes values in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mrow
                          %mn 0
                          %mo ,
                          %mn ∞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {[{0,\infty}[}
            %li
              A measure is
              %strong bounded
              if, for some (finite) real number
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\mu(S)| \leq M
              for every measurable set
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              have a real-valued notion of absolute value or norm, so a vector-valued measure should be valued in a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+space"} Banach space
              or something similar.)
            %li
              A measure is
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
                \-finite
              if every measurable set is the union of countably many sets with finite measure.
          %p Remarks:
          %ul
            %li
              The property that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              is increasing holds for all positive measures but may fail for others.
            %li
              A positive measure
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              that satisfies
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(X)=1
              must be a probability measure as defined earlier; that is, it satisfies
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(S) \leq 1
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %li
              When
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              is allowed as a value of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
            %li
              Notice that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\infty
              is not allowed as a value for a signed measure. It would work just as well to allow
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\infty
              and forbid
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo +
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\infty + \infty
          %p
            Another possibility is to generalize the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/source"} source
            of
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "-ring" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed "-ring." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+space"} measurable space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            is a
            = succeed "-ring," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            in this way is complementary to generalizing the target above; in particular, it may allow one to avoid dealing with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-algebra," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %em finite
            positive measure on the
            = succeed "-ring" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            = succeed "-ring" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            = succeed "-finite" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
          %p
            Yet another possibility is to drop countable additivity, replacing it with finite additivity. The result is a
            = succeed "," do
              %strong finitely additive measure
            %strong charge
            to avoid the
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/red+herring+principle"} red herring principle
            = succeed "." do
              %strong countably additive
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            with an algebra (or even a ring) of sets; again, see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+space"} measurable space
            for these definitions.
          %p
            Finally, an
            %strong extended measure
            takes values in the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-\infty,\infty]
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+real+numbers"} extended real numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(S)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(T)
            must be finite if they have opposite signs and
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi T
                    %mo =
                    %mi ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \cap T = \empty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\infty
            cannot both be values of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            after all). To deal with this, we define an extended measure to be the formal difference
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi μ
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mi μ
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu^+ - \mu^-
            of positive measures;
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi μ
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %msup
                    %mi μ
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(S) = \mu^+(S) - \mu^-(S)
            whenever this is not of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo −
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty - \infty
            and is otherwise undefined. Note that the set of extended measures on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+set"} quotient set
            of the set of pairs of positive measures; we say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo =
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu = \nu
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi ν
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(S) = \nu(S)
            whenever either side is defined, that is if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            are the same as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+functions"} partial functions
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-\infty,\infty]
          %p
            Any extended measure restricts to an infinite signed measure, taking
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma'\subset\Sigma
            to be the set of elements of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            with a finite measure. Vice versa, an infinite signed measure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            canonically extends to an extended measure: we can define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_+
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_-
            as usual and then take the formal difference
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mo −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_+-\mu_-
          %h3#pointfree_measure_spaces Point-free measure spaces
          %p
            Just like how in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point-free+topology"} point-free topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opens"} opens
            to be fundamental and defines a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
            as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame+of+opens"} frame of opens
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-complete+Boolean+algebra"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
              \-complete Boolean algebra
            of measurables.
          %p
            Then the various notions of measures and measure spaces above also make sense on a
            = succeed "-complete" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+set"} countable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/union"} union
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection"} intersection
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+subsets"} measurable subsets
            in a traditional measurable space are replaced with the countable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/join"} join
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meet"} meet
            of measurables in the
            = succeed "-complete" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed "-complete" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            is a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo :
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu:\Sigma \to [0, 1]
            such that
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The measure of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/top+element"} top element
                is
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/one"} one
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ⊤
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(\top) = 1
            %li
              %p
                Countable additivity:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi ℕ
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo =
                          %mn 0
                        %mn ∞
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(\bigvee_{i \in \mathbb{N}} S_i) = \sum_{i=0}^{\infty} \mu(S_i)
                whenever
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi i
                      %mo ∧
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi j
                      %mo =
                      %mo ⊥
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_i \wedge S_j = \bot
                for all
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \in \mathbb{N}
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j \in \mathbb{N}
                where
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ≠
                        %mi j
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \neq j
          %h3#constructive_theory Constructive theory
          %p
            In Henry Cheng's
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            theory of measure, the definition of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+space"} measurable space
            becomes more complicated; the main point is that a single measurable set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is replaced by a
            %em complemented pair
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S,T)
          %p
            In the requirements (1–3), the constants
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \empty
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and the operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \union
            are interpreted by formal
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Morgan+duality"} de Morgan duality
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cheng+measurable+space"} Cheng measurable space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(S,T)
            is the measure of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            alone, as follows:
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(S,T) = \mu(S,U)
              whenever
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S,T)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S,U)
              are both complemented pairs.
          %p
            In general, a
            %em measurable set
            is any set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S,T)
            is a complemented pair for some set
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %p We will mention other occasional fine points in the constructive theory when they occur; the main outline does not change.
          .query
            %p I need to check Bishop &amp; Bridges to see if there are any other changes, but I don't think so; that is, I went through the following, and it all seems correct as it is. —Toby
          %h3#measures_on_a_frame
            Measures on a
            = succeed "-frame" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
          %p
            In measure theory, a
            %strong measure
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-frame"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
              \-frame
            or more generally a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-complete+lattice"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
              \-complete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattice"} distributive lattice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ≤
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∨
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊤
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L, \leq, \bot, \vee, \top, \wedge, \Vee)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valuation+%28measure+theory%29"} valuation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo :
                  %mi L
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu:L \to [0, \infty]
            such that the elements are mutually disjoint and the probability valuation is denumerably/countably additive
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi s
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mi ℕ
                  %mo .
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi m
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo .
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo ≠
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall s\in L^\mathbb{N}. \forall m \in \mathbb{N}. \forall n \in \mathbb{N}. (m \neq n) \wedge (s(m) \wedge s(n) = \bot)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi s
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mi ℕ
                  %mo .
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo :
                      %mi ℕ
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo :
                      %mi ℕ
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall s\in L^\mathbb{N}. \mu(\Vee_{n:\mathbb{N}} s(n)) = \sum_{n:\mathbb{N}} \mu(s(n))
          %h3#subsidiary_definitions Subsidiary definitions
          %p
            Given a measure space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\Sigma,\mu)
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              \-null
              = succeed "-measurable" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            is a measurable set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(S) = 0
            whenever
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subseteq N
            is measurable; a
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              \-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/null+set"} null set
            is any subset of a null measurable set. In a positive measure space, we don't have to bother with
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            will be a null measurable set as long as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(N) = 0
          %p
            A
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              \-full
              = succeed "-measurable" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            is a measurable set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(S) = \mu(S \cap F)
            for every measurable set
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              \-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+set"} full set
            is any superset of a full measurable set. In a probability measure space, we don't have to bother with
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            will be a full measurable set as long as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(F) = 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
            of a null set, but this doesn't hold in the constructive theory.
          %p
            A property of elements of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            holds
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              \-almost everywhere
            if the set of values where it holds is full.
          %p
            A measure is
            %strong complete
            if every full set is measurable. We may form the
            %strong completion
            of a measure space by accepting as a measurable set the intersection of any set and a full set; these
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              \-measurable sets
            will automatically form a
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            originally was). Classically, a measure is complete if and only if every null set is measurable and a set is
            = succeed "-measurable" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+difference"} symmetric difference
            between a measurable set and a null set.
          %p
            A
            %strong
              finitely
              = succeed "-measurable" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            is a measurable set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(S)
            is finite whenever
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subseteq M
            is measurable; a
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
              \-finitely
              = succeed "-measurable" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            is any union of countably many finitely measurable sets. Again, we don't have to bother with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            in a positive measure space. Note that a measure space is (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            )-finite if and only if every measurable set is (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            )-finitely measurable. The finitely measurable sets form a
            = succeed "-ring," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            = succeed "-finitely" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed "-ring." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
          %p
            Recall that a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+function"} measurable function
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\Sigma)
            to some other measurable space is any function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            such that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preimage"} preimage
            under
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            of a measurable set is always measurable (or something more complicated in the constructive theory). Now that we have a measure space, let a
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              \-measurable function
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+function"} partial function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to some other measurable space such that the domain of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is full and the preimage under
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            of a measurable set is always
            = succeed "-measurable" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              \-equivalent
            if their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equaliser"} equaliser
            is a full set. We are really interested in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+set"} quotient set
            under this equivalence and so identify equivalent
            = succeed "-measurable" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            = succeed "-measurable" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            = succeed "-measurable" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
          %p
            A
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              \-integrable function
            is a
            = succeed "-measurable" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            such that the integral
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi S
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_S f \,\mu
            (as defined below) exists for every measurable set
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo =
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S = X
            = succeed "-measurable" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            such that the extended measure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \mu
            (also defined below) is actually a finite measure. (In any case, we get a finite measure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \mu
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is integrable.)
          %h3#integration Integration
          %p
            In the following, ‘measurable’ will mean
            = succeed "-measurable." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            is complete and identify
            = succeed "-equivalent" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            is a positive measure until I make sure of what must be done to generalise.
          %p
            Given a measure
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi S
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_S f \,\mu
          %p
            (see
            %a{:href => "#notation"} above
            for variations in notation) in stages, from the simplest form of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            to the most arbitrary.
          %p
            Each measurable subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subseteq X
            induces a measurable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+function"} characteristic function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi S
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_S\colon X \to \mathbb{R}_+
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_S(x) = 1
            if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_S(x) = 0
            if
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in \neg{S}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is a complemented pair,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_S
            is a partial function with a full domain, so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi S
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_S f
            is still a measurable function as defined above.) In general, we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi S
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi μ
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi S
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_S f \,\mu = \int_X \chi_S f \,\mu ,
          %p
            so from now on we will assume that we are integrating over all of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (and drop the subscript).
          %p
            A positive
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+function"} simple function
            is a finite
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}_+
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∫
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi i
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi μ
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi i
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int \sum_{1 \leq i \leq n} a_i \chi_{S_i} \,\mu = \sum_{1 \leq i \leq n} a_i \mu(S_i) .
          %p
            The integral is extended to all measurable functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X \to [0, \infty]
            by the rule
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi μ
                  %mo =
                  %mi sup
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi s
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi μ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi s
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi s
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi simple
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int f \,\mu = \sup \{ \int s \,\mu \;|\; 0 \leq s \leq f, s\; simple \}
          %p
            if this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supremum"} supremum
            converges. Classically, the integral either converges or diverges to infinity, so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int f \,\mu
            exists in some sense in any case; the possibilities are more complicated constructively.
          %p
            For any measurable function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X \to [-\infty, \infty]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_+
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{-}
            by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi max
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi max
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_+(x) = \max\{f(x), 0\}, \qquad f_{-}(x) = \max\{-f(x), 0\}
          %p
            so that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = f_+ - f_{-}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|f|} = f_+ + f_{-}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi μ
                  %mo =
                  %mo ∫
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi μ
                  %mo −
                  %mo ∫
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int f \,\mu = \int f_{+} \,\mu - \int f_{-} \,\mu
          %p
            if both integrals on the right converge. Classically, the other possibilities are
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\infty
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo −
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty - \infty
          %p
            A measurable function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is
            %strong integrable
            with respect to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            if this integral converges. It can be proved that all of the definitions above are consistent; that is, if the final definition is applied to a simple function, then it agrees with the original definition.
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            takes values in the field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            or in some more general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+space"} Banach space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|f|}
            is integrable. If it is, then we say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %strong absolutely integrable
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi f
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mo ≤
                  %mo ∫
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi μ
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|\int f \,\mu\|} \leq \int {\|f\|} \,\mu .
          %p
            This integral is easy to define if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            has a basis; for example, a measurable complex-valued function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X \to \mathbb{C}
            is integrable iff both its real and imaginary parts are integrable, and we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi μ
                  %mo =
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi ℜ
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi μ
                  %mo +
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                  %mo ∫
                  %mi ℑ
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi μ
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int f \,\mu = \int \Re{f} \,\mu + \mathrm{i} \int \Im{f} \,\mu .
          .query
            %p
              I need to check
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HAF"} HAF
              for more details here in the general case. In particular, something can be integrable without being absolutely integrable (although not if it's complex-valued, of course) or indeed even without being valued in a (pseudo)normed space.
          %p
            The vector space of
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi f
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo ∫
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi μ
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|f\|_1} \coloneqq \int {\|f\|} \,\mu .
          %p Note that we must use the notion of measurable function as an equivalence class of functions to get a Banach space here; otherwise we have only a pre-Banach space (that is, a complete pseudonormed vector space).
          %p
            This Banach space is called a
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+space"} Lebesgue space
            and is denoted
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi μ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^1(\mu,V)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^1(X,V)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is usually either
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^p
            also exist;
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^p
            precisely when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi f
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|f|^p}
            is integrable, and we use
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi f
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi p
                  %mo ≔
                  %mroot
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mi f
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                          %mi p
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi μ
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|f\|_p} \coloneqq \root p {\int {\|f\|^p} \,\mu}
          %p
            as the norm. (At least, this is so for
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∈
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in {]{0,\infty}[}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+space"} Lebesgue space
            for generalizations to other values of
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %h2#the_algebra_of_measures The algebra of measures
          %p Note that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo ∫
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi S
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f \mu) (S) = \int \chi_S f \,\mu
          %p
            makes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \mu
            into a
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is an integrable
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-\infty,\infty]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            is a signed measure, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is an extended measure which is finite iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is integrable. We have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi μ
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f g) \mu = f (g \mu) .
          %p Thus integration can be seen as a way of multiplying a function by a measure to get another measure.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Radon-Nikodym+derivative"} Radon-Nikodym derivative
            is about reversing this (dividing two measures to get a function).
          %p Other topics: absolute continuity, etc. (Refer to &lt;http://tobybartels.name/notes/#Radon&gt;.)
          %h2#noncommutative_measure_theory Noncommutative measure theory
          %p
            Every commutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebra"} von Neumann algebra
            is isomorphic to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+space"} Lebesgue space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^\infty(X,\mu)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            is some measure (which is irrelevant) on a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localisable+measurable+space"} localisable measurable space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+category"} duality
            between localisable measurable spaces and commutative von Neumann algebras. This is similar to the correspondence between commutative
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} algebras
            and
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+compact+Hausdorff+space"} locally compact Hausdorff space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counting+measure"} counting measure
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haar+measure"} Haar measure
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+measure"} Borel measure
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Radon+measure"} Radon measure
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gaussian+measure"} Gaussian measure
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+measure"} spectral measure
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wiener+measure"} Wiener measure
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The pointless version of the notion of measurable space is the notion of
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+locale"} measurable locale
            %li
              %p
                In the context of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+integration"} fiber integration
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology"} generalized cohomology
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation+in+generalized+cohomology"} orientation in generalized cohomology
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density+of+a+subset"} density of a subset
                can be considered as taking the measure of sets normally considered to be not well behaved in measure theory, such as infinite subsets of the natural numbers.
          %h2#references References
          %p
            See the references at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
          %p
            For measures on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-frame"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
                \-frames
          %ul
            %li
              Alex Simpson,
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0168007211001874"} Measure, randomness and sublocales
          %p
            Discussion via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+toposes"} Boolean toposes
            is in
          %ul
            %li#Jackson06
              %p
                Matthew Jackson,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A sheaf-theoretic approach to measure theory
                = succeed ")" do
